<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is a Nightingale chart?</span></em></strong>=
</h4>
<p>A Nightingale chart is a variation of a pie chart. It also consists of s=
ectors, but with different radiuses. In this chart, the value corresponds t=
o the area of the sector, not the radius.&nbsp;</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a Nightingale chart?</span></em><=
/strong></h4>
<p>A Nightingale chart is visually similar to the pie chart, but it has a d=
ifferent function. It=E2=80=99s used mostly for comparison.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a Nightingale chart</span></em=
></strong></h4>
<p>In most cases, a Nightingale chart is not the best primary chart choice.=
 It=E2=80=99s really hard to compare the sector areas of a Nightingale char=
t precisely. It also inherits all the issues of the pie chart. The most obv=
ious is that it=E2=80=99s hard to distinguish sectors with similar values. =
For these types of cases, it=E2=80=99s better to use bar charts.&nbsp;</p>
