<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is a quadrant chart?</span></em></strong></h=
4>
<p>A quadrant chart is very similar to a scatter plot but it=E2=80=99s divi=
ded into equal parts (quadrants) in a matrix view. It can be 2x2, 3x3, 4x4 =
or other ratios, depending on the aim of the visualization.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a quadrant chart?</span></em></st=
rong></h4>
<p>It is useful if you want to group distinct data marks for some specific =
type of analysis. One of the best and most well-known examples of using the=
 quadrant chart is for a SWOT analysis. The quadrant chart is useful for be=
nchmarking if there are two dimensions that should be taken into account.&n=
bsp;</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn=E2=80=99t use a quadrant chart</span=
></em></strong></h4>
<p>In case the splitting into quadrants doesn=E2=80=99t make sense or looks=
 forceful.</p>
