data-visualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-=
size: 14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
<a id=3D"contour-plot" data-hs-anchor=3D"true"></a><hr>
<h3><br>Contour plot<br><br><img src=3D"https://www.datylon.com/hs-fs/hubfs=
/Datylon%20Website2020/Blogs/80%20types%20of%20charts%20and%20graphs/charts=
%20iso%20links/80%20types%20of%20charts%20examples%20%E2%80%93%20Contour%20=
plot.png?width=3D600&amp;height=3D600&amp;name=3D80%20types%20of%20charts%2=
0examples%20%E2%80%93%20Contour%20plot.png" width=3D"600" height=3D"600" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" alt=3D"80 types of charts examples =E2=80=93 Contour plot" s=
tyle=3D"height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 600px; margin-left: auto; mar=
gin-right: auto; display: block;"><br><span></span></h3>
<h4><strong><em><span>What is a contour plot?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A contour plot shows three numerical variables on a flat page. Two varia=
bles form the x=E2=80=91axis and y=E2=80=91axis, just like a scatter plot. =
The third variable appears as curved lines that link every point with the s=
ame value. Picture a topographic map: one line marks 200=E2=80=AFm above se=
a level, the next marks 300=E2=80=AFm, and so on. Wherever the lines crowd =
together, the value changes quickly, and where they spread apart the surfac=
e is flatter. If you fill the spaces between the lines with colour, the plo=
t reads like a smooth relief map of the data.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a contour plot?</span></em></stro=
ng></h4>
<p>Contour plots shine when you need to show a continuous surface. For exam=
ple, elevation on a landscape or temperature across a weather map. Their fl=
owing lines help readers see gradients quickly. This is why they are common=
 in cartography, meteorology, oceanography, and other scientific fields tha=
t map smooth phenomena such as density, pressure, or magnetic strength. How=
ever, they can also suit business or retail data if you want to highlight d=
ense clusters without clutter.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a contour plot</span></em></st=
rong></h4>
<p>Avoid a contour plot if the audience is not used to reading contour line=
s. It takes practice to translate the spacing of the lines into a sense of =
magnitude. The method also assumes that the data vary smoothly. If the surf=
ace is patchy or if there are sharp jumps, the interpolated lines can be mi=
sleading. Contour plots require enough data to create reliable interpolatio=
n, so they are not suitable for small or widely spaced samples.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Finally, if a simpler chart such as a heatmap can communicate the story =
clearly, prefer the simpler option for ease of interpretation.</p>
