<h4><br><em><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">What is a pie chart?</span><=
/em></h4>
<p>A pie chart is a circular graphic for showing how a total breaks into pa=
rts. The full circle represents the whole, while each slice represents a ca=
tegory=E2=80=99s share of that whole. The slices must add up to a meaningfu=
l total, which is often 100=E2=80=AFpercent. If your data are absolute numb=
ers such as dollars or units, all categories together should equal a sensib=
le total.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a pie chart?</span></em></strong>=
</h4>
<p>Use a pie chart when you have only a few categories and you want readers=
 to see their relative sizes instantly. It is especially effective when one=
 slice is much larger or smaller than the others, because the difference st=
ands out immediately.&nbsp;</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a pie chart</span></em></stron=
g></h4>
<p>Avoid a pie chart when you need precise comparisons. Human vision judges=
 angles and curved areas poorly, so small differences between slices are ha=
rd to read. The chart is also limited if you have many categories, because =
narrow wedges become crowded and labels overlap. For detailed or multi=E2=
=80=91category comparisons, a bar chart, stacked bar, or another linear cha=
rt will communicate the data more clearly.</p>
