uto; max-width: 100%; width: 600px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; =
display: block;"><br></em></p>
<h4><strong><em><span>What is a circular treemap?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A circular treemap uses circles instead of rectangles to show a hierarch=
y. Each top=E2=80=91level category is drawn as a large circle, and these ca=
n be divided into smaller circles for sub=E2=80=91categories. The area of e=
very circle matches the value it represents, so larger values appear as lar=
ger circles within the overall packing.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a circular treemap?</span></em></=
strong></h4>
<p>Choose a circular treemap when you want to display a hierarchy in a way =
that feels organic and visually engaging. Although it is less space=E2=80=
=91efficient than a rectangular treemap, a well=E2=80=91designed circle pac=
king can be eye=E2=80=91catching and easy for viewers to explore. It works =
best for overviews where the exact proportions are less critical and visual=
 appeal is a priority.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a circular treemap</span></em>=
</strong></h4>
<p>Avoid a circular treemap when precise comparison matters more than showi=
ng the hierarchy. People judge circle areas less accurately than rectangle =
sizes or bar lengths, so fine differences between values can be hard to spo=
t. If space is tight or you need exact comparisons, a rectangular treemap o=
r a bar chart will serve better.</p>
