<h3><br>Dendrogram</h3>
<p>Alternative name: <em>Phylogenetic tree<br><br><img src=3D"https://www.d=
atylon.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Datylon%20Website2020/Blogs/80%20types%20of%20charts=
%20and%20graphs/charts%20iso%20links/80%20types%20of%20charts%20examples%20=
%E2%80%93%20Dendrogram.png?width=3D600&amp;height=3D600&amp;name=3D80%20typ=
es%20of%20charts%20examples%20%E2%80%93%20Dendrogram.png" width=3D"600" hei=
ght=3D"600" loading=3D"lazy" alt=3D"80 types of charts examples =E2=80=93 D=
endrogram" style=3D"height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 600px; margin-lef=
t: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;"><br></em></p>
<h4><strong><em><span>What is a dendrogram?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A dendrogram is a tree=E2=80=91shaped diagram that shows how items clust=
er into a hierarchy. It begins with each item as an individual leaf; branch=
es then merge step by step until all items join a single trunk. The height =
where two branches join represents the distance between those items. <a hre=
f=3D"https://www.displayr.com/what-is-dendrogram/"><span>Clusters near the =
bottom reflect the raw data most directly</span></a> and are the most relia=
ble. As you move up the tree the merges rely on earlier averages, so the re=
lationships become progressively less precise.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a dendrogram?</span></em></strong=
></h4>
<p>Use a dendrogram when you need to present the results of hierarchical cl=
ustering. Biologists rely on it to map evolutionary links among species, wh=
ile other fields use the same tree layout for gene clustering, customer seg=
mentation, or grouping similar documents. The branching structure lets read=
ers see which clusters lie close together and where the major splits occur.=
</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a dendrogram</span></em></stro=
ng></h4>
<p>Avoid a dendrogram if the hierarchy is shallow or the dataset is huge. T=
oo few levels give a tree that could be shown more clearly as simple groups=
. On the contrary, hundreds of leaves produce a tangle of lines that is har=
d to read. If your main goal is to compare cluster sizes or values, use a b=
ar chart or heatmap instead.</p>
<p><a href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-=
data-visualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-=
size: 14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
<a id=3D"Venn-diagram" data-hs-anchor=3D"true"></a><hr>
<h3><br>Venn diagram</h3>
<p>Alternative name: <em>Set diagram</em>, <em>Logic diagram<br><br></em></=
p>
<h4><strong><em><span><img src=3D"https://www.datylon.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Datyl=
on%20Website2020/Blogs/80%20types%20of%20charts%20and%20graphs/charts%20iso=
%20links/80%20types%20of%20charts%20examples%20%E2%80%93%20Venn%20diagram.p=
ng?width=3D600&amp;height=3D600&amp;name=3D80%20types%20of%20charts%20examp=
les%20%E2%80%93%20Venn%20diagram.png" width=3D"600" height=3D"600" loading=
=3D"lazy" alt=3D"80 types of charts examples =E2=80=93 Venn diagram" style=
=3D"height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 600px; margin-left: auto; margin-=
right: auto; display: block;"><br><br>What is a Venn diagram?</span></em></=
strong></h4>
<p>A Venn diagram uses overlapping circles to show every possible intersect=
ion among two or more sets, even when some intersections contain no items. =
Shared regions reveal the elements that the sets have in common. Non=E2=80=
=91overlapping regions hold elements unique to each set. Because the layout=
 includes empty overlaps as well as real ones, it presents the full logical=
 picture of how the sets could relate.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a Venn diagram?</span></em></stro=
ng></h4>
<p>Choose a Venn diagram when you want a familiar, easy=E2=80=91to=E2=80=91=
read picture of how two or three sets relate, including places where they c=
ould overlap but do not. This makes it useful in lessons, presentations, an=
d simple reports where highlighting both real and potential intersections h=
elps explain the concept.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a Venn diagram</span></em></st=
rong></h4>
<p>Skip a Venn diagram if you have more than three sets or if you care only=
 about the overlaps that actually exist. Extra circles or empty intersectio=
ns quickly make the graphic cluttered. In those situations, an Euler diagra=
m, which shows only real overlap, will be clearer.</p>
<p><a href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-=
data-visualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-=
size: 14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
<a id=3D"Euler-diagram" data-hs-anchor=3D"true"></a><hr>
<h3><br>Euler diagram</h3>
<h4><strong><em><span><br><img src=3D"https://www.datylon.com/hs-fs/hubfs/D=
atylon%20Website2020/Blogs/80%20types%20of%20charts%20and%20graphs/charts%2=
0iso%20links/80%20types%20of%20charts%20examples%20%E2%80%93%20Euler%20diag=
ram.png?width=3D600&amp;height=3D600&amp;name=3D80%20types%20of%20charts%20=
examples%20%E2%80%93%20Euler%20diagram.png" width=3D"600" height=3D"600" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" alt=3D"80 types of charts examples =E2=80=93 Euler diagram" =
style=3D"height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 600px; margin-left: auto; ma=
rgin-right: auto; display: block;"><br><br>What is an Euler diagram?</span>=
</em></strong></h4>
<p>An Euler diagram (pronounced =E2=80=9COY=E2=80=91ler=E2=80=9D) looks a l=
ot like a Venn diagram, but it shows only the overlaps that actually exist =
in the data. In a Venn diagra, every possible intersection appears, even if=
 no items fall in those intersections. An Euler diagram omits any empty int=
ersections. So each region of the drawing corresponds to a real=E2=80=91wor=
ld set or overlap and nothing more.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use an Euler diagram?</span></em></st=
rong></h4>
<p>Use an Euler diagram to show only the overlaps that truly exist. It work=
s well for two or three sets that share some items yet stay distinct elsewh=
ere. This lets readers focus on real connections without empty intersection=
s.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use an Euler diagram</span></em></=
strong></h4>
<p>Do not use an Euler diagram when you must display every possible interse=
ction, even ones that are empty. It is also limited when you have many sets=
 or very complex overlaps, as the visual gets messy. In those situations, c=
hoose a matrix chart instead.</p>
