<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is an area chart?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>An area chart builds on a line chart. You plot data points, connect them=
 with a line, and then shade the space between the line and the x=E2=80=91a=
xis. The filled area shows how a total grows or shrinks over time, so the r=
eader sees volume as well as trend.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use an area chart?</span></em></stron=
g></h4>
<p>Choose an area chart when you want to show the magnitude of a measure, n=
ot just its trend. The filled space draws the eye to how much has accumulat=
ed or changed over time. This is helpful for totals that start at zero, suc=
h as monthly revenue, website visits, or rainfall. Pick an area chart when =
the story is about =E2=80=9Chow much=E2=80=9D in addition to =E2=80=9Cgoing=
 up or down.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use an area chart</span></em></str=
ong></h4>
<p>Avoid an area chart when you need to compare precise values or show smal=
l fluctuations. In this case, a simple bar chart is better to use. Moreover=
, the filled shape can hide fine detail, and if the data does not start at =
zero, the shaded area can mislead. In this case, a line chart is clearer.<b=
r><br><a href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-exampl=
