<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is a stream graph?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A stream graph is undoubtedly one of the most beautiful chart types avai=
lable. This stunning type of chart is a variation of a stacked area chart. =
Instead of piling layers on a fixed x=E2=80=91axis, it balances them around=
 a central baseline. The layers rise and fall like a flowing river, so the =
chart looks smooth and organic. Peaks and dips show how each series grows o=
r shrinks over time, and the overall shape can hint at seasonal patterns.</=
p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a stream graph?</span></em></stro=
ng></h4>
<p>Choose a stream graph when you want to highlight broad trends in multiva=
riate time=E2=80=91series data and add visual appeal. It works well for soc=
ial=E2=80=91media analysis over months, web=E2=80=91traffic sources through=
 a year, stock=E2=80=91market sectors, or climate indicators. The floating =
baseline reduces the eye=E2=80=99s focus on exact numbers and lets viewers =
see how the mix of categories shifts over time.</p>
<h4><span style=3D"font-size: 21px;"><strong><em><span style=3D"color: #434=
343;">When you shouldn't use a stream graph</span></em></strong></span></h4=
>
<p>Avoid a stream graph if precise comparisons matter. Because layers rest =
on a moving baseline, it is hard to read exact values or small differences.=
 The chart also becomes confusing when you have many thin layers or highly =
volatile data. In those cases, a standard stacked area chart, a grouped lin=
e chart, or small multiples will be clearer.<br><br><a href=3D"https://www.=
datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-data-visualization#table-o=
f-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-size: 14px;">Back to table=
 of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
