<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is a Gantt chart?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A Gantt chart is a bar chart that maps tasks against time. The y=E2=80=
=91axis lists activities or tasks, and each task is drawn as a horizontal b=
ar whose left end marks the start date and whose right end marks the finish=
 date. The x=E2=80=91axis shows the calendar, so you can see instantly when=
 each task begins, how long it lasts, and where tasks overlap.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a Gannt chart?</span></em></stron=
g></h4>
<p>Use a Gantt chart to plan or track work that unfolds over time. It is a =
staple in project management for monitoring schedules, deadlines, and progr=
ess, but the format suits any data that describes time blocks. You can plot=
 crop seasons, service availability, or animal migration periods with the s=
ame layout. Any situation where =E2=80=9Cwhat happens when=E2=80=9D is the =
key question is a good fit.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a Gannt chart</span></em></str=
ong></h4>
<p>Skip a Gantt chart if the horizontal axis is not time. When the bars rep=
resent values instead of dates, use a range bar chart instead. Also, avoid =
a Gantt if you need to compare the exact duration of tasks; a simple bar ch=
art that shows length alone will make those comparisons clearer.<br><br><a =
href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-data-v=
isualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-size: =
14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
