<h4><strong><em><span><br>What is a strip plot?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>A strip plot is a variation of a scatter plot, but it only has one categ=
orical and one numerical axis. It is a chart used to illustrate the distrib=
ution of many individual one-dimensional values. These values look like tra=
nsparent dots located along a single (category) axis. If some of the dots h=
ave the same value, they can overlap, creating something that looks like a =
strip. Unlike a histogram and a density plot, the values are not placed in =
bins. Distribution is shown here via the density of overlapping dots.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a strip plot?</span></em></strong=
></h4>
<p>A strip plot works well for showing the distribution of several categori=
es. It works best when the number of values is under 20-30 and there=E2=80=
=99s no intense overlapping of dots. A strip plot is also useful when you w=
ant to show the exact values. Another advantage of this plot is its space e=
fficiency.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a strip plot</span></em></stro=
ng></h4>
<p>When the number of values exceeds 30, the overlapping of dots might look=
 overwhelming and not informative, even if they are transparent.<br><br><a =
href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-data-v=
isualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-size: =
14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
