rgin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;"><br></em></p>
<h4><strong><em><span>What is a beeswarm chart?</span></em></strong></h4>
<p>In a beeswarm chart, dots are used to show the distribution of a variabl=
e. Values are each represented by one dot, and the swarm of dots represents=
 the distribution found in the data. Instead of packing them in bins, the d=
ots are scattered around each other and plotted on a single axis. This kind=
 of chart is very useful when you want to display a lot of data points at o=
nce.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When should you use a beeswarm chart?</span></em></st=
rong></h4>
<p>If you want to use dots to show the distribution of a variable and it's =
important that dots won=E2=80=99t overlap, a beeswarm chart is your choice.=
 The additional advantage of the beeswarm chart is that you can assign an a=
dditional variable to the size of the dot.</p>
<h4><strong><em><span>When you shouldn't use a beeswarm chart</span></em></=
strong></h4>
<p>Beeswarm charts are constructed in a way that the dots are placed with s=
ome approximation. It means that the position of every dot might not be acc=
urate. It means if the position of every dot is important to you, you shoul=
d choose another chart: a histogram, a density plot or a strip plot.<br><br=
><a href=3D"https://www.datylon.com/blog/types-of-charts-graphs-examples-da=
ta-visualization#table-of-contents" rel=3D"noopener"><span style=3D"font-si=
ze: 14px;">Back to table of contents =E2=AC=86=EF=B8=8F</span></a></p>
